# Prismatic cohomology, Part II: target signatures

The roadmap is `README.md`; this file is not the roadmap. It states the roadmap's
headline theorems against the pinned Mathlib and Tau Ceti, each with `sorry`, and
nothing else. No headline theorem of this roadmap is statable at the pins: the
prismatic and cohomological carriers are absent, so nothing is stated in their place.
The headline theorems and the carriers each lacks are:

* Layer 3: the Nygaard graded-piece comparisons (§§3.5, 3.13, 3.15), canonical
  Breuil–Kisin twists (§§3.8–3.9), Frobenius factorization through `Lη_I` (§3.16),
  the de Rham comparison for every bounded prism (§3.17), the Frobenius isogeny
  (§3.18), and the Hodge, completion and coordinate comparisons (§§3.19–3.26).
  These require prisms, relative prismatic cohomology, its filtered derived
  Nygaard object, the canonical twist and derived décalage.
* Layer 4: the Cartier–Witt crystal description (§4.10), Hodge–Tate divisor and
  Sen classification (§§4.12–4.14), Frobenius pullback square (§4.16), absolute
  site, crystalline and de Rham comparisons (§§4.19–4.24), and absolute Nygaard
  and divided-Frobenius theorems (§§4.29–4.31). These require the Cartier–Witt
  stack, its enhanced quasi-coherent complexes, the Sen operator and absolute
  prismatic cohomology with its twists and filtrations.
* Layer 5: the q-crystalline/crystalline and prismatic comparisons (§§5.11–5.13),
  multiplicative q-Poincaré and q-de Rham comparisons and framing independence
  (§§5.18–5.21), the `AΩ` comparison and its specializations (§§5.23–5.24),
  comparison uniqueness (§5.25), and the Nygaard coordinate formula (§5.26).
  These require q-PD pairs and envelopes, q-crystalline cohomology, completed
  framed q-de Rham complexes, `AΩ` and the prismatic functor with its
  Hodge–Tate structure map.

Witt vectors, ideals, invertible modules, classical adic completion and derived
categories are available, but constructing the missing objects requires the
mathematical developments specified in the layers.
-/

